WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.487 --> 00:00:02.320
SPEAKER: When creating
data product clusters

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.570
for using open source
big data tools,

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:06.580
such as Spark,
Hadoop, or Presto,

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.500
it is imperative that
they are configured

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.210
with well-defined
security settings

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.480
to prohibit unauthorized use
and access to the cluster.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.280
Google Cloud contains
several options

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.870
for how to control the security
settings on your cluster.

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.540
So let's jump in and
learn more about this.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:24.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.484 --> 00:00:30.810
In this video, we'll
be going over how

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.510
to secure your
Dataproc clusters using

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.050
a custom virtual
private cloud, or VPC.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.260
A VPC is a managed networking
service for your Google Cloud

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.550
resources.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:45.300
Resources within your VPC
are secured via isolation

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.980
from the public internet.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.410
For more info on
virtual private cloud,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.710
check out the video
link in the description.

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.330
We'll start by
setting up the cluster

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.450
to allow the cluster nodes
to connect to one another.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.210
The cluster cannot distribute
workloads to its nodes without

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:00.990
this.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:05.099
Additionally, we'll also allow
SSH access into the cluster.

00:01:05.099 --> 00:01:07.590
SSH is important for
submitting workloads

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.070
with tools such as Spark Submit
and for making modifications

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:12.330
to long-running clusters.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.540
We can do this by creating a
custom VPC with two firewall

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.820
rules, one that allows
the nodes of the cluster

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.710
to interact with each
other and another

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.920
that enables SSH access
into the Dataproc cluster.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.930
These rules will provide
the Dataproc cluster

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.970
with basic security features.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.090
Next, we'll create the
cluster with our custom VPC.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.490
We'll then test the
functionality of the VPC

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.120
by submitting a Spark job to
the cluster using the Cloud SDK,

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.970
as well as show how to
SSH into the cluster.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.460
We'll then delete the
individual firewall rules

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.710
and show how we protected
the cluster and the job

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:44.610
successfully.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.830
Without the firewall
rules, we are locked out,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.410
and the cluster is
effectively rendered unusable.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.760
Yay.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.530
You can jump ahead
to any of these steps

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:56.580
by visiting the chapters in
the YouTube description below.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.610
Start by searching for VPC
network in the search bar,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:00.570
and click on it.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.960
Next, click Create VPC Network.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.130
Provide a name for
the VPC network.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.100
Next, we'll create a subnet
using the Custom Subnet

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.690
Creation mode.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.870
Provide a name for
your new subnet.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.100
Select a region.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.710
In this case, we'll
pick US Central 1.

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.390
Provide an IP address.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:30.570
We'll go with 10.0.0.0/9.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.110
Leave the rest of the default
as is, and press Create.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:35.940
This should take
about 20 seconds or so

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.491
to finish creating.

00:02:37.491 --> 00:02:41.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.560
When it's finished
creating, click on it.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:48.310
Click on Firewall Rules,
then click Add Firewall Rule.

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:53.490
We'll first create
a firewall rule

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.050
that will allow
nodes of the cluster

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.790
to communicate with each other.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:00.660
Provide a name for the rule.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.650
Make sure the name of
your network is selected.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:07.070
Make sure the direction of
traffic is set to Ingress.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:12.200
For target tags, provide
the name dataproc-network.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:19.895
For source IP ranges,
input 10.0.0.0/9.

00:03:19.895 --> 00:03:24.380
For protocols and ports,
check the box next to TCP,

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:31.700
and enter the ports 0-65535
to allow access on all ports.

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:36.170
Also, check the box next
to UDP and input All.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:36.755
Press Create.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.000
You'll see the new
firewall rule here.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.880
Now, let's create our
second firewall rule

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.880
by again clicking
Add Firewall Rule.

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:52.140
This firewall rule will allow
SSH access to the cluster.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:55.010
Provide a name for the rule.

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:58.340
Make sure the name of
your network is selected.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:03.860
For target tags, again provide
the name dataproc-network.

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:12.320
For source IP ranges,
input 0.0.0.0/0.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.640
For protocols and ports,
check the box next to TCP,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.800
and enter the port 22, which
is the port used for SSH login.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:22.400
Press Create.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.190
We'll now create
our Dataproc cluster

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:31.240
configured with our VPC.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.800
In the search bar, type in
Dataproc, and click on it.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.630
Next, click Create Cluster.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:42.640
Provide a name.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.480
Select the same
region as your VPC.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.970
Leave everything else
in this tab as is.

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:53.290
Next, select Customize Cluster.

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:56.890
Under Network Configuration, for
the primary network dropdown,

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:00.070
select your network, and for
the sub network dropdown,

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.450
select your subnet.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.500
Under Network Tags, enter the
same tags you created earlier,

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.070
dataproc-network.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:10.040
Everything else
will be left as is.

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:11.210
Go ahead and press Create.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.640
We see our cluster is
provisioning, which will take

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:18.560
about 90 seconds to finish.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.010
We'll skip the video ahead here.

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.470
Now that our cluster has
finished provisioning, let's

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:28.070
try submitting a job to it
using the Dataproc jobs API.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:30.770
Click on the Cloud Shell button
to open up your Cloud Shell.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.500
Now we'll copy this
command into the terminal

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:37.840
to submit an example
job to Spark.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.840
In this case, will you Spark
Pi, which will approximately

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.055
compute the value of pi.

00:05:45.890 --> 00:05:46.490
Great.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:47.300
This worked.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.730
We can see the success
message in the output.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:53.630
Now, we'll go back to our VPC
and remove the firewall rule.

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.625
Again, search for VPC network.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:08.870
Click on my-vpc-network-demo,
and then firewall rules.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:11.840
Check the box next to
the cluster-coms rule,

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:12.920
then press Delete.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.930
Now, we'll try submitting
the Spark job again

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:21.430
in our Cloud Shell.

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.680
After about 60
seconds, it will fail.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.089
We'll again skip
the video ahead.

00:06:26.089 --> 00:06:30.001
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.380
As you can see, the cluster
failed to execute the job,

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:40.050
as it wasn't able to communicate
with the other nodes.

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.630
This means having the
rule there worked.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:45.420
Go back to the Dataproc
page by typing Dataproc

00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.620
into the search bar.

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:54.190
Click on the cluster, then
click the VM Instances tab.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.118
Next to the master
node, press SSH.

00:06:57.118 --> 00:07:00.106
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.340
We're now SSH'ed
into the cluster.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.820
Let's see if we're moving the
firewall rule for SSH prevents

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.500
us from doing this again.

00:07:11.500 --> 00:07:15.035
Close this SSH window, and now
let's search for VPC network

00:07:15.035 --> 00:07:15.535
again.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:24.980
Click on my-vpc-network-demo,
and then firewall rules.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:28.190
Check the box next to
the allow-ssh rule,

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.360
then press Delete.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.270
Go back to the Dataproc
page by typing Dataproc

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:38.190
into the search bar.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:42.120
From the Clusters page,
click on the cluster.

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:46.360
Click on the VM Instances tab.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.475
Next to the master
node, click SSH.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.630
This process should hang
for about a minute or so

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:57.300
before unsuccessfully
connecting.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.220
We'll skip the video again here.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:06.990
This means the allow-ssh
firewall rule worked, as well.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.800
In this video, we
created a VPC network

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:13.160
with two firewall rules
in place to limit access.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:16.340
We then created a Dataproc
cluster configured with the VPC

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.530
network to secure
it via isolation

00:08:18.530 --> 00:08:20.370
from the public internet.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.730
We then tested the
functionality of the cluster

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:25.190
and showed this
functionality disappear as we

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:27.260
removed the firewall rules.

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.480
You can read more about
network configuration

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:32.510
for your Dataproc cluster
in the Google Cloud Docs.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.710
Please go to this link
for more information.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.320
Thanks for watching.

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:39.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

